WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.920
Does each taste fibre carry one taste, or must the brain read a pattern across many

2
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:08.920
fibres?

3
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:13.943
If each fibre listened only to cells of one taste, it would be a labelled line.

4
00:00:14.843 --> 00:00:16.403
Its activity would mean one taste.

5
00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:22.517
In anaesthetized mice, Barretto and colleagues imaged geniculate ganglion neurons

6
00:00:22.517 --> 00:00:24.557
while tastants flowed over the tongue.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:31.907
Most responding neurons were strongly activated by only one of the tested tastes.

8
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.400
One class did not fit.

9
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.600
Some neurons responded to both bitter and sour.

10
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:43.590
Two ideas. Maybe the neuron gets input from both sour and bitter cells.

11
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.860
Or it listens only to bitter cells, and acid also activates them.

12
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.853
The authors silenced the sour cells.

13
00:00:52.753 --> 00:00:55.713
Under each idea, what should happen to the bitter-sour neurons?

14
00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.827
Sour-only responses disappeared.

15
00:01:04.507 --> 00:01:06.567
But the bitter-sour class was still there.

16
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.770
And when bitter responses were blocked instead, the bitter-sour class disappeared.

17
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:20.210
So the authors concluded that acid drives these neurons through bitter cells.

18
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.410
For these neurons, the fibre reports the cell, not the chemical.

19
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:26.623
Now the counter-weight.

20
00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:30.443
Wu and colleagues compared low and higher concentrations.

21
00:01:31.563 --> 00:01:33.403
Tuning broadened as concentration rose.

22
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:39.153
Of the neurons tested at both strengths, most became more broadly tuned at the

23
00:01:39.153 --> 00:01:40.153
higher one.

24
00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:41.993
So narrow or broad?

25
00:01:43.013 --> 00:01:47.653
Imaging usually calls a neuron responsive when its signal crosses a fixed threshold.

26
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:53.513
Picture a neuron with a full input from its main cell type, and a weak one from a

27
00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:54.513
second.

28
00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:57.607
Each input saturates as concentration rises.

29
00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:00.527
These numbers are made up, to show the logic.

30
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.630
At low strength, does the weak input cross the threshold?

31
00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.410
And at high strength?

32
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.360
At low strength, the main input crosses, and the weak one stays below.

33
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.260
The neuron looks narrow.

34
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.400
At high strength, the weak input climbs past the line.

35
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.620
The same neuron now looks broad.

36
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:35.193
In this model, a weak input of weight 0.2 never reaches the threshold of 0.25.

37
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:40.973
Whenever the main response crosses that threshold, the neuron responds to one taste

38
00:02:40.973 --> 00:02:41.993
in this panel only.

39
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.350
So in the model, neurons with different weak inputs differ.

40
00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.650
Some broaden and some do not.

41
00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.270
Wu found most broadened, but not all.

42
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.500
Those data fit the model, but they do not show which neurons have weak inputs.

43
00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:04.390
If responses were counted against the neuron's own best, and all inputs rose alike,

44
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:05.950
breadth would not change.

45
00:03:06.933 --> 00:03:10.653
So narrow depends on the panel, the concentration and the criterion.

46
00:03:11.593 --> 00:03:14.053
The two laboratories' results need not conflict.
